is a global HTTP, HTTP(S) Load

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:43.464
Balancing, SSL Proxy,
TCP Proxy, and then

00:00:43.464 --> 00:00:45.380
we've got two other
flavors that are regional,

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:48.840
which is Network LB
and Internal L4 LB.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.810
And one of the things that many
of you know but some of you may

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.810
not, is that these load
balancing flavors actually

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:58.380
underpin a lot of things,
especially load balancing

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.960
on the computer engine side--
or whether it's Cloud Storage--

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:02.580
or whether it's
the GK side, which

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:05.055
is the Kubernetes
side, or even yourself,

00:01:05.055 --> 00:01:08.130
manage Kubernetes on GCP.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:11.010
So we thought, let's start
with the basics, which was

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:12.390
the first flavor that we have.

00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:14.704
And then Mike, give us a tour
of Network Load Balancer.

00:01:14.704 --> 00:01:15.870
MIKE COLUMBUS: Sure, thanks.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.030
So around two years
ago, we published

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:20.610
a paper that revealed
the architectural details

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.230
of the Maglev.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:24.340
Now, the Maglev has
been around for a while.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:26.490
It's our in-house
developed, all software load

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:30.090
balancer that runs on
standard Google hardware.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.010
This load balancer
has been handling

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.060
the majority of Google's
traffic since 2008

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.740
and is a key part
of our architecture.

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:41.190
Maglevs handle traffic as they
come into our data centers.

00:01:41.190 --> 00:01:44.100
They direct traffic across
our front end infrastructure.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:47.040
And they're also exposed
to you as our Network Load

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.830
Balancer for Google Cloud.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:52.710
So the Network Load Balancer,
when you create a network load

00:01:52.710 --> 00:01:55.140
balancer, we use
forwarding rules

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.000
that basically
direct TCP and UDP

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.640
traffic across regional
back ends that can

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.827
be auto scaled using hashing.

00:02:02.827 --> 00:02:04.410
So it's a network-based
load balancer.

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:07.650
We look at the source/dest
port IP and protocol.

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:09.270
And you can, you
know, manipulate

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:12.210
the hashing algorithm
as you see fit.

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:13.740
When you create a
forwarding rule,

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:16.230
you get a VIP, or a
Virtual IP address,

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:21.050
that originates from a
regional network block

00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:24.630
wherever you tie that forwarding
rule to your back ends.

00:02:24.630 --> 00:02:29.220
So this VIP is anycast out
our global points of presence,

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.755
but the back ends are regional,
meaning you cannot have back

00:02:31.755 --> 00:02:35.040
ends that span multiple regions.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.140
This is a pass
through load balancer,

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.200
so your back ends are going
to see the original client

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.280
request.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:44.750
It's not doing any TLS
offload or proxying.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:47.665
So traffic gets directly
routed to your VMs.

00:02:47.665 --> 00:02:49.290
And that's where you
can use the Google

00:02:49.290 --> 00:02:53.430
firewall to control or filter
access to the back end VMs.

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:57.600
So in this diagram, you see we
have three regional back ends,

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:59.020
three forwarding rules.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.170
We have Maya in California,
Bob in New York,

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.000
and Shen in Singapore.

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:05.730
And they're all connecting into
their back end resources, which

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:09.000
is my app test and travel.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:12.540
But if Shen were to connect
into the US-West back end,

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:15.570
again, would ingress that
traffic closest to Shen

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:19.350
in Singapore because that
ranges anycast out our network.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:20.850
And then we would
route that traffic

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:24.010
to the regional back end.

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:26.620
So let's take a little
bit of a deeper look

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:28.480
under the hood of the Maglev.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.950
So on the left here, you see a
traditional middle edge proxy

00:03:32.950 --> 00:03:35.710
load balancing design
which are typically

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:38.290
deployed in active
standby pairs, or highly

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:39.760
available pairs.

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.856
They're scaled up as
more capacity is needed.

00:03:42.856 --> 00:03:45.430
And typically, if you have
massive spikes in traffic,

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:47.260
this needs to be
pre-warmed to prepare

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:49.420
for the influx of traffic.

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.951
On the right hand, you can
see the Maglev architecture.

00:03:51.951 --> 00:03:53.950
So again, this is an all
software-based solution

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:55.910
that runs on our
standard servers

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:57.910
and it's horizontally scalable.

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:01.100
The way this works is,
in a traditional sense,

00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:05.000
a load balancer is primary
or active for its VIP, right?

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.330
In the Maglev architecture,
we announce all VIPs

00:04:07.330 --> 00:04:09.400
to our edge routers via ECMP.

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:13.030
So we'll distribute
flows based on hashing--

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:14.620
so equal cost routing--

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:16.339
to our Maglevs.

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:19.630
The Maglevs will then
use consistent hashing.

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:22.450
What this means is as
Maglevs, or load balancers

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:25.030
are added to the pool,
we'll get consistent results

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:26.980
as that back end is selected.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:29.620
The Maglevs then
encapsulate that traffic

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:32.490
based on the virtual
network ID for the customer

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:34.150
and send that to the
back end service.

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:38.140
And then we use direct server
return to send that traffic

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:40.360
directly into our edge routers.

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:45.910
So the Maglevs are not in
path for the return flow.

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:49.630
So I want to recap what features
we have with our network load

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:50.680
balancer.

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:52.910
Again, it's a regional solution.

00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:55.270
So we have high availability
by deploying your back ends

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:57.070
in multiple zones.

00:04:57.070 --> 00:04:58.840
It does not look at Layer 7.

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.530
It load balances TCP and UDP.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:04.270
The back ends will see the
original client request.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.700
So there's no need to pass
an x-forwarded-for header,

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:09.610
use proxy protocol to
inform the back ends of what

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:13.060
the original client request was.

00:05:13.060 --> 00:05:17.020
And you can control this
access with our VPC firewall.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:19.660
We balance based on
2, 3, or 5-tuple.

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:24.130
And you can get affinity by
using 2 or 3-tuple hashing

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:25.720
so that way the
source, if you're

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:27.940
using source IP, Dest
IP, you'll always

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:30.350
choose the same back end.

00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:31.600
We health check the back ends.

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:33.860
And this is a very high
performance solution.

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:35.200
So we published a paper.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:39.490
We handled 1 million requests
per second with no pre-warming.

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:40.630
What it does not provide.

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:45.310
So there is no global back ends
for the Network Load Balancer.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:47.230
We also only support IPv4.

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:50.790
So there's no IPv6 support
for the Network Load Balancer.

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:54.680
No Layer 7 traffic routing,
and also no TLS termination

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:56.440
or offload.

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.922
So with that, I'll hand
it over to Prajakta.

00:05:58.922 --> 00:06:00.130
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Thanks, Mike.

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:02.740
So that is how-- so we had
the network load balancer.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:04.180
And then, given
all of the reasons

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:06.430
that Mike spoke
through, that's how

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:09.220
we decided to go on and
build a Global Load Balancer.

00:06:09.220 --> 00:06:12.190
Now, most of you are familiar
with the kind of load balancing

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:14.350
that you see in
other public clouds.

00:06:14.350 --> 00:06:16.240
And the load balancers
are regional.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:18.340
And what that means
is, per region,

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:21.070
you need a virtual IP
for your load balancer.

00:06:21.070 --> 00:06:24.650
And then your back ends are
simply in that region as well.

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:26.290
So now imagine you
wanted to set up

00:06:26.290 --> 00:06:27.896
your service in three regions.

00:06:27.896 --> 00:06:29.770
And then you would need
to go put three VIPs.

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:32.190
And then, obviously, you
need a DNS Load Balancer

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:35.980
or a Global Server Load Balancer
to go pick one of those VIPs

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:37.540
for your given client.

00:06:37.540 --> 00:06:39.950
And this has actually
several issues.

00:06:39.950 --> 00:06:43.260
So as an example, if
something were to go down,

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:45.190
first of all, your
DNS load balancer

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:48.080
has to go and understand
something went down.

00:06:48.080 --> 00:06:50.950
The second thing is your
local DNS to the client

00:06:50.950 --> 00:06:53.050
could go and cache the
IPs, and that could

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:55.210
result in suboptimal selection.

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:57.040
And the other thing
is, imagine you ran out

00:06:57.040 --> 00:06:59.040
of capacity in one region.

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:00.280
The regions are silos.

00:07:00.280 --> 00:07:02.530
So the load balancer in
one region cannot leverage

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:05.320
the capacity in another region.

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:08.300
This obviously would not have
worked for Google Services.

00:07:08.300 --> 00:07:09.820
And so this is when
we thought, why

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:13.210
don't we rethink how global
load balancing is done?

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:16.510
And the idea was to not depend
on the DNS load balancer

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:18.610
to go do load balancing for you.

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:20.710
So now imagine,
first of all, you

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:22.090
don't build out
the load balancer

00:07:22.090 --> 00:07:25.030
using managed VMs, or
instances, or servers.

00:07:25.030 --> 00:07:27.940
Instead, we just put all
of Google's Global Edge

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:31.130
infrastructure and we put
load balancing logic there.

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:33.370
And this was along with a
bunch of different systems

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:36.460
that interoperate to do load
balancing for your clients.

00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:39.190
Which means it doesn't matter
where you put your application

00:07:39.190 --> 00:07:41.770
instances, you get a
single front end VIP.

00:07:41.770 --> 00:07:43.810
You get worldwide
capacity because now

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:45.820
that VIP is fronting
all of your capacity

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:47.627
across all of your deployments.

00:07:47.627 --> 00:07:49.210
And then you get
cross region failover

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:50.840
and so on and so forth.

00:07:50.840 --> 00:07:53.260
And one of the really nice
things about the Load Balancer

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:56.150
is that it's very tightly
coupled with our auto

00:07:56.150 --> 00:07:59.260
scaler, which means
as your traffic grows,

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:01.270
it will scale up your
back end instances.

00:08:01.270 --> 00:08:04.880
And then as your traffic ebbs,
it'll scale them down as well.

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:07.090
It's also-- one really
nice side effect

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:10.360
of it is the VIP becomes,
or our global VIP becomes

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:13.600
a very nice place to go apply
all your global policies.

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:16.250
And the other thing is you
don't have a choke point,

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:19.120
because these are not just
point implementations with VMs

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:20.630
and so on.

00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:22.462
So a really nice
way to think about,

00:08:22.462 --> 00:08:24.670
you know, what does this
Global Load Balancing really

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:25.790
look like?

00:08:25.790 --> 00:08:27.940
So often we have customers
who will start off

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:31.300
with a single region and they
will go deploy back ends there.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:33.900
They will configure
the load balancing VIP.

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:36.730
Now, it's very likely
that as your service grows

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:39.580
in popularity, you will
need to bring up application

00:08:39.580 --> 00:08:41.350
instances in another region.

00:08:41.350 --> 00:08:44.860
And so as you can see here, once
Bob in New York comes on board,

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:47.290
all you need to do is go
add application instances

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:49.170
in a region in US-East.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:50.545
You don't need to
change the VIP.

00:08:50.545 --> 00:08:53.003
You don't need to put a DNS
load balancer because you still

00:08:53.003 --> 00:08:54.100
have only one VIP.

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:57.380
In this case, it's 200111.

00:08:57.380 --> 00:08:59.360
And then you can
just expand wherever

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:02.630
Google has data centers by
simply putting instances there.

00:09:02.630 --> 00:09:04.940
But as you can notice, there
was no change to the VIP.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:07.070
There was no change
to your DNS server.

00:09:07.070 --> 00:09:09.920
And you definitely did not
need any of the things related

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:13.360
to DNS load balancing.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:15.190
One of the other things
which I spoke about

00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:17.290
was the capacity that you get.

00:09:17.290 --> 00:09:19.210
So in this case, for
instance, let's say

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:21.600
your instances in
Singapore are overloaded.

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:24.580
So Shen in Singapore would
be very seamlessly directed

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:27.100
to any of the other
available instances.

00:09:27.100 --> 00:09:29.770
And it's also possible that
the California instances

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:31.510
for your app are overloaded.

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:34.960
And so your users could be
overflowed back into New York.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:36.850
And once the capacity
is available--

00:09:36.850 --> 00:09:39.490
because, basically, the
autoscaler scaled up-- then

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:42.490
the clients would
be brought back.

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:44.800
So a lot of people ask us,
like, what does this look

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:47.020
like under the hood?

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:50.050
So because of all of our
mental models of load balancers

00:09:50.050 --> 00:09:51.640
are these boxes,
this is actually

00:09:51.640 --> 00:09:54.370
what the global load balancer
looks like under the hood.

00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:55.690
We have a tiered edge.

00:09:55.690 --> 00:09:58.240
So we do part of the load
balancing and termination

00:09:58.240 --> 00:09:59.536
at our Tier 1.

00:09:59.536 --> 00:10:01.285
And then we do some
of the load balancing,

00:10:01.285 --> 00:10:04.600
the remainder of the load
balancing logic at the Tier 2.

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:07.030
So that's what you see, the
two tiered architecture.

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:08.650
And then your
application instances

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:11.500
are in the last, which
says instances there.

00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:13.620
That's where you would be
running your application

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:14.120
software.

00:10:16.421 --> 00:10:17.920
The other thing
that a lot of people

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:20.620
ask us is, so you have
all of these instances,

00:10:20.620 --> 00:10:23.602
and how does the load balancer
go distribute traffic?

00:10:23.602 --> 00:10:25.435
What it does is it fills
up the local region

00:10:25.435 --> 00:10:27.610
to capacity for a given client.

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:31.380
Then it'll fill up basically
all the zones within a region.

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:33.870
Then, if you're out of
capacity in a region,

00:10:33.870 --> 00:10:36.070
it will overflow to
the closest region.

00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:40.470
And then, within the zone
itself, it round-robins.

00:10:40.470 --> 00:10:41.959
You can totally
forget all of this

00:10:41.959 --> 00:10:43.750
because you don't need
to worry about this.

00:10:43.750 --> 00:10:45.460
This happens automatically.

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:48.930
But the key takeaway is as long
as you have available capacity

00:10:48.930 --> 00:10:50.680
in the region that's
closest to your user,

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:52.390
your user will land up there.

00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:54.610
You will overflow your
failover only if there's

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:57.010
some issue, or capacity
issue, or availability

00:10:57.010 --> 00:11:01.340
issue in the region
closest to the user.

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:03.090
The other thing that
we wanted to walk you

00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:05.580
through is, this is
what's under the hood.

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:08.160
So what does the data model
for the Global Load Balancer

00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:09.120
look like?

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:10.810
So you have essentially
the VIP, which

00:11:10.810 --> 00:11:12.750
is the global forwarding route.

00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:13.975
Then you have the proxy.

00:11:13.975 --> 00:11:15.930
So if you're running
HTTP traffic,

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:16.950
your proxy will be HTTP.

00:11:16.950 --> 00:11:20.700
Or it's HTTP(S) or SSL,
or any of the other TCP

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:22.200
and so on and so forth.

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:24.250
Then you've got the
back end side of it.

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:26.499
This is where you're actually
putting your application

00:11:26.499 --> 00:11:27.000
instances.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:29.880
And so a service maps
to a back end service.

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:31.830
And then that could have
application instances

00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:34.410
within instance groups
in one or more regions,

00:11:34.410 --> 00:11:36.840
depending on where
you are users are.

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:38.700
So this was a whirlwind tour.

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:39.830
But what I wanted to do.

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:44.590
Mike, why don't you take us on
a tour of this through a demo?

00:11:44.590 --> 00:11:46.480
MIKE COLUMBUS: I'd be happy to.

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:47.257
Thanks, Prajakta.

00:11:47.257 --> 00:11:48.840
So today we're going
to be interfacing

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:52.290
with an HTTP(S) Global
Load Balancer Deployment.

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:54.060
It's live up and running.

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:55.710
And I wanted to
take a few minutes

00:11:55.710 --> 00:11:58.710
to talk through the components
and how they're configured,

00:11:58.710 --> 00:12:01.680
and really solidify the object
model that Prajakta just

00:12:01.680 --> 00:12:02.996
spoke of.

00:12:02.996 --> 00:12:05.370
And what's really cool is if
you're one of the select few

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:09.420
who are online during this
conference on the network,

00:12:09.420 --> 00:12:12.390
you can connect to this fancy
front end we built for you.

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:16.190
So if we can switch to the demo.

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:16.690
All right.

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:18.840
So here we're in
Google's Cloud Console.

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:21.517
We're going to navigate
to Network Services.

00:12:21.517 --> 00:12:23.100
And this is where
we'll configure load

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:25.860
balancing DNS and CDN.

00:12:25.860 --> 00:12:27.960
In this exercise,
we'll be taking a look

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:28.890
at our load balancers.

00:12:28.890 --> 00:12:30.640
You can see we have
three in this project.

00:12:30.640 --> 00:12:32.400
The first two
we'll get to later.

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:34.650
The third one is what we're
going to go through today.

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:36.749
This is an HTTP(S)
Load Balancer.

00:12:36.749 --> 00:12:38.790
So we're going to drill
in a little bit on what's

00:12:38.790 --> 00:12:41.380
configured.

00:12:41.380 --> 00:12:45.090
So in the Cloud Console, it
really breaks this object model

00:12:45.090 --> 00:12:47.200
down into three components.

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:50.270
The first is the
front end, right?

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:52.347
Host and path rules,
which is basically like,

00:12:52.347 --> 00:12:54.180
we'll look at the URL
request and route that

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:56.251
to the appropriate
back end service.

00:12:56.251 --> 00:12:57.500
And then the back end service.

00:12:57.500 --> 00:13:01.150
I'll go through each component
in its configuration here.

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:05.352
So we just spent a bunch of
time talking about a single VIP.

00:13:05.352 --> 00:13:06.060
There's two here.

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:08.160
You might be saying,
Mike, what gives?

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:11.850
It's because the
first VIP is for IPv4.

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:14.130
The second VIP is for IPv6.

00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:19.200
So basically, for the domain
name, www.gcpnetworking.com,

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:21.430
we're going to take
in HTTP(S) traffic

00:13:21.430 --> 00:13:24.960
and direct this to
our target proxy.

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:30.120
So when you create an HTTP(S)
front end global forwarding

00:13:30.120 --> 00:13:32.710
rule, you need to
have a certificate.

00:13:32.710 --> 00:13:34.382
So we support SNI.

00:13:34.382 --> 00:13:35.959
But I kind of cheated here.

00:13:35.959 --> 00:13:37.500
This is a little
bit of a sneak peek.

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:38.890
I used a Google Manage cert.

00:13:38.890 --> 00:13:42.360
So this is going to manage the
lifecycle of the cert for me--

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:43.170
super easy.

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:46.320
I just, you know, when
I deployed the cert,

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:47.880
I configured the domain.

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:51.060
And using Cloud
DNS, I pointed an A

00:13:51.060 --> 00:13:56.190
and quad A record to these IP
addresses, and it deployed.

00:13:56.190 --> 00:13:58.290
SSL policy we'll get
to it a little bit.

00:13:58.290 --> 00:14:00.690
We're going to
additively do, further

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:03.300
in this talk, some additional
things to this environment.

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:04.667
We'll secure the environment.

00:14:04.667 --> 00:14:06.000
And the network tier is premium.

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:07.041
This is our default tier.

00:14:07.041 --> 00:14:09.690
This means that
these VIP ranges come

00:14:09.690 --> 00:14:13.110
from a global pool of
addresses that will anycast out

00:14:13.110 --> 00:14:14.730
our global pops, right?

00:14:14.730 --> 00:14:17.400
So kind of back to the DNS thing
that Prajakta mentioned, right?

00:14:17.400 --> 00:14:22.190
One A record, one quad A record,
route clients via anycast,

00:14:22.190 --> 00:14:25.894
just BGP routing to
the closest front end.

00:14:25.894 --> 00:14:28.060
So from there, we'll talk
about host and path rules.

00:14:28.060 --> 00:14:32.030
So in this environment,
it's pretty simple.

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:34.750
You don't need to configure
these for the HTTP(S) Load

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:35.260
Balancer.

00:14:35.260 --> 00:14:36.220
It's kind of important, right?

00:14:36.220 --> 00:14:38.530
That's why you might want
a Layer 7 Load Balancer.

00:14:38.530 --> 00:14:41.380
What these do is that we
look at the incoming URL

00:14:41.380 --> 00:14:43.750
and direct the traffic to
the appropriate back end

00:14:43.750 --> 00:14:45.740
service based on this matcher.

00:14:45.740 --> 00:14:47.320
So you can do path matches.

00:14:47.320 --> 00:14:49.700
In this environment,
it's pretty simple.

00:14:49.700 --> 00:14:52.390
Let me zoom in here further.

00:14:52.390 --> 00:14:54.070
Make it a little easier to see.

00:14:54.070 --> 00:14:57.490
So anything unmatched will go
to our Web back end service.

00:14:57.490 --> 00:15:01.030
Anything slash secure is going
to go to our Secure back end

00:15:01.030 --> 00:15:02.380
Service.

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:04.190
The back end
services themselves.

00:15:04.190 --> 00:15:06.730
So these are made up of a
collection of instance groups.

00:15:06.730 --> 00:15:08.410
The instance groups
can be deployed

00:15:08.410 --> 00:15:10.060
in any one of our 20 regions.

00:15:10.060 --> 00:15:14.005
In this environment,
I picked US-West 1--

00:15:14.005 --> 00:15:17.500
it's fairly close--
and Europe-West 1.

00:15:17.500 --> 00:15:19.990
But in addition to adding
your instance groups

00:15:19.990 --> 00:15:21.640
into your back end
service, it's also

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:22.891
a collection of configuration.

00:15:22.891 --> 00:15:25.556
So you're going to want to group
your applications appropriately

00:15:25.556 --> 00:15:27.710
into back end services
for this reason.

00:15:27.710 --> 00:15:30.190
So the first thing that's
configured in the back end

00:15:30.190 --> 00:15:32.080
services is your
endpoint protocol.

00:15:32.080 --> 00:15:34.820
So we pitched TLS everywhere.

00:15:34.820 --> 00:15:36.400
I was a little bit lazy.

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:37.870
I did HTTP.

00:15:37.870 --> 00:15:39.934
But it's fairly common
to use self-signed certs

00:15:39.934 --> 00:15:41.350
in the back end
because, you know,

00:15:41.350 --> 00:15:44.260
your clients are connecting
into the front end proxy.

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:45.760
You'll notice the named port.

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:48.130
Where this is important--
and this is a configuration

00:15:48.130 --> 00:15:50.140
that's part of the
instance group--

00:15:50.140 --> 00:15:52.730
is that this is
basically a key pair.

00:15:52.730 --> 00:15:56.020
So if you wanted the Load
Balancer to direct traffic

00:15:56.020 --> 00:15:58.900
to your instances on the
back end on, you know,

00:15:58.900 --> 00:16:02.650
a nonstandard port, you'll set
a named port in the instance

00:16:02.650 --> 00:16:04.180
group, associate
that to the port

00:16:04.180 --> 00:16:06.970
that you want to
receive traffic on.

00:16:06.970 --> 00:16:10.180
And the back ends will receive
traffic from the front end

00:16:10.180 --> 00:16:13.500
or from the Google Load
Balancer on that port.

00:16:13.500 --> 00:16:14.990
Timeout is also very important.

00:16:14.990 --> 00:16:17.915
So if you're running web sockets
or long lived HTTP connections,

00:16:17.915 --> 00:16:19.540
you're probably going
to want a timeout

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:20.860
of greater than 30 seconds.

00:16:20.860 --> 00:16:25.010
This is a fairly static page so
I didn't muck with this at all.

00:16:25.010 --> 00:16:26.170
We enabled Cloud CDN.

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:28.910
It's a simple check box in your
back end service configuration.

00:16:28.910 --> 00:16:30.934
So if you had, you
know, dynamic content

00:16:30.934 --> 00:16:32.350
that you didn't
want to cache, you

00:16:32.350 --> 00:16:33.730
could leave it
off for a back end

00:16:33.730 --> 00:16:35.480
service associated with that.

00:16:35.480 --> 00:16:38.782
And then, you know, enable
it for static content.

00:16:38.782 --> 00:16:40.240
The other thing I
forgot to mention

00:16:40.240 --> 00:16:43.270
is in your host
and path rules, you

00:16:43.270 --> 00:16:46.150
can also direct to back
end service or also

00:16:46.150 --> 00:16:47.390
a back end bucket.

00:16:47.390 --> 00:16:49.960
So I wanted to just
mention that real quick.

00:16:49.960 --> 00:16:51.980
The back end
service health check

00:16:51.980 --> 00:16:54.860
is configured on the
back end service.

00:16:54.860 --> 00:16:58.420
So we're using a basic HTTP
check for our back end services

00:16:58.420 --> 00:17:00.265
to make sure that
they're healthy.

00:17:00.265 --> 00:17:02.140
And then there's some
advanced configuration.

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:04.675
So we can configure session
affinity, connection draining,

00:17:04.675 --> 00:17:05.540
or timeout.

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:07.077
So basically how
long if we drain,

00:17:07.077 --> 00:17:09.160
or if we're going to be
removing a VM, how long do

00:17:09.160 --> 00:17:11.460
we want to keep those
connections active.

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:13.069
We can associate
a security policy.

00:17:13.069 --> 00:17:14.569
It's a little bit
of a teaser there.

00:17:14.569 --> 00:17:15.970
We'll do that later.

00:17:15.970 --> 00:17:19.504
And also identity aware
proxy, which we'll talk about.

00:17:19.504 --> 00:17:21.670
The other thing is, you can
also add custom headers.

00:17:21.670 --> 00:17:24.290
So if we wanted
to insert a header

00:17:24.290 --> 00:17:28.600
or have the HTTP(S)
Load Balancer inform us

00:17:28.600 --> 00:17:30.970
of say, geolocation
or some custom header,

00:17:30.970 --> 00:17:34.270
we can insert those on
a per back end service.

00:17:34.270 --> 00:17:35.740
So this back end
service consists

00:17:35.740 --> 00:17:38.410
of three instance groups.

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:41.496
You'll see everything's
healthy, which is great.

00:17:41.496 --> 00:17:42.620
You got to love live demos.

00:17:42.620 --> 00:17:44.620
We have back end.

00:17:44.620 --> 00:17:48.610
We have an instance group in
Europe-West 1 and US-West 1.

00:17:48.610 --> 00:17:50.320
You'll notice we
set the auto scaling

00:17:50.320 --> 00:17:56.530
to 80% of our target, which is
300 requests per second per VM.

00:17:56.530 --> 00:17:58.900
So auto scaling
will be triggered,

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:02.260
in this case, 80% of
300, which is 240,

00:18:02.260 --> 00:18:04.036
assuming that capacity is 100%.

00:18:04.036 --> 00:18:05.410
This is an additional
knob that's

00:18:05.410 --> 00:18:07.480
really important for
Canary or A/B releases.

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:12.062
So you might notice this new
web, you know, instance group.

00:18:12.062 --> 00:18:14.020
This is really like if
I want to spin up a test

00:18:14.020 --> 00:18:17.760
and direct a subset of traffic
to this instance group.

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:19.590
I set the capacity for 10%.

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:22.990
So 10% of 300, so about
30 requests per second

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:24.460
will go to this
new instance group.

00:18:24.460 --> 00:18:27.950
So great for Canary
and additional control.

00:18:27.950 --> 00:18:33.310
Now, the next thing I want to do
is contrast the Load Balancing

00:18:33.310 --> 00:18:35.740
configuration with the
configuration of an instance

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:36.360
group itself.

00:18:36.360 --> 00:18:39.730
So we'll pick an instance
group, which is US-West 1.

00:18:39.730 --> 00:18:42.700
And let me just zoom
out a little bit.

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:43.994
And we'll edit this group.

00:18:43.994 --> 00:18:45.535
So in the instance
group itself, here

00:18:45.535 --> 00:18:47.180
is where you specify
the key pair.

00:18:47.180 --> 00:18:49.300
So we're mapping
HTTP to port 80.

00:18:49.300 --> 00:18:51.800
We could do, you know, 8080 on
the back end if we wanted to,

00:18:51.800 --> 00:18:54.070
or 8443.

00:18:54.070 --> 00:18:58.420
This instance group is built
off a template, which basically

00:18:58.420 --> 00:19:00.220
calls in a custom
image, trying to keep

00:19:00.220 --> 00:19:02.940
the boot time relatively short.

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:06.100
We have auto scaling on based
on HTTP(S) Load Balancing usage.

00:19:06.100 --> 00:19:10.646
We can also use CPU
usage or custom metrics.

00:19:10.646 --> 00:19:12.286
But we want the auto scaler.

00:19:12.286 --> 00:19:13.910
Because we see the
requests per second,

00:19:13.910 --> 00:19:16.540
we want the auto scaler
to trigger auto scaling.

00:19:16.540 --> 00:19:19.569
We have a minimum of one
instance, a maximum of 10.

00:19:19.569 --> 00:19:21.610
And the cool down period
is relatively important.

00:19:21.610 --> 00:19:23.109
What this means is
it's a time we're

00:19:23.109 --> 00:19:25.500
going to wait before we
collect statistics on instance.

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:27.820
So if you have long-running
startup scripts,

00:19:27.820 --> 00:19:30.195
you're going to want to know
how long your instance takes

00:19:30.195 --> 00:19:33.200
to boot and set appropriately
in your cool down period.

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:36.510
The other thing is auto healing.

00:19:36.510 --> 00:19:39.730
So auto healing is the ability
to have an additional health

00:19:39.730 --> 00:19:42.130
check that will recreate
the VM if it goes

00:19:42.130 --> 00:19:44.470
unhealthy or it's unresponsive.

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:46.420
Typically, your auto
healing health check,

00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:48.550
you want to relax a
little bit because it's

00:19:48.550 --> 00:19:51.870
a more expensive
operation to reboot a VM

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:54.730
or rebuild a VM versus
just taking it out

00:19:54.730 --> 00:19:57.780
of the healthy available
pool for your back end.

00:19:57.780 --> 00:19:59.560
But that's there.

00:19:59.560 --> 00:20:02.814
And it's turned on here with
an initial delay of 30 seconds.

00:20:02.814 --> 00:20:04.480
This is similar to
the cool down period.

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:06.730
We're going to wait 30
seconds before we health check

00:20:06.730 --> 00:20:09.250
this back end VM.

00:20:09.250 --> 00:20:11.410
So we've gone through
the configuration.

00:20:11.410 --> 00:20:15.790
Another thing I have set
up here is some load.

00:20:15.790 --> 00:20:20.970
So I want to show just a little
bit of monitoring our Load

00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:21.780
Balancer.

00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:24.290
So jump back in here.

00:20:28.970 --> 00:20:30.600
So here's our web
back end service.

00:20:30.600 --> 00:20:33.320
I have two load generators,
one in North America, one

00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:34.760
in Europe.

00:20:34.760 --> 00:20:37.010
Both are sending about
1,000 requests per second.

00:20:37.010 --> 00:20:40.160
And what this graph shows
us is that traffic arriving

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:43.610
at our North American
front end locations

00:20:43.610 --> 00:20:47.450
are always being directed
to our US-West 1 back end

00:20:47.450 --> 00:20:50.480
service or instance groups.

00:20:50.480 --> 00:20:53.060
Now, this goes to the
distribution, right?

00:20:53.060 --> 00:20:55.760
We're always going to select
the healthiest back end

00:20:55.760 --> 00:20:57.429
with available capacity.

00:20:57.429 --> 00:20:58.970
You can see a small
subset of traffic

00:20:58.970 --> 00:21:01.190
is being directed to
our Canary release

00:21:01.190 --> 00:21:04.670
site, which is our new web
back end instance group.

00:21:04.670 --> 00:21:06.470
And then in Europe,
all those requests

00:21:06.470 --> 00:21:08.830
are being served by the
Europe-West 1 instance group.

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:14.100
And then the last thing
we'll do is, let's just--

00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:16.520
we can connect to the demo site.

00:21:16.520 --> 00:21:19.380
So hopefully some of you
were able to access this.

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:21.080
So it's a pretty
simple web page.

00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:24.130
The client IP is the
original client IP so

00:21:24.130 --> 00:21:25.940
originating from
probably some NAT Gateway

00:21:25.940 --> 00:21:27.200
here at the Moscone.

00:21:27.200 --> 00:21:29.960
Load Balancer IP is
the Load Balancer VIP.

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:32.790
The proxy IP is
basically the connection,

00:21:32.790 --> 00:21:35.930
so the actual IP address that
the VM back end will see once

00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:37.695
that connection is proxied.

00:21:37.695 --> 00:21:39.320
And these are known
ranges of addresses

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:41.695
that you're going to want to
allow through your firewall,

00:21:41.695 --> 00:21:42.505
your VM firewalls.

00:21:42.505 --> 00:21:45.050
You can see the host name
we're connecting into.

00:21:45.050 --> 00:21:46.220
We're in San Francisco.

00:21:46.220 --> 00:21:47.540
We're connecting to US-West 1.

00:21:47.540 --> 00:21:49.790
I'd be a little alarmed
if this was Europe.

00:21:49.790 --> 00:21:52.580
And you can see the
region and zone.

00:21:52.580 --> 00:21:54.650
So with that, let's
jump back to the slides.

00:21:54.650 --> 00:21:56.991
Let's talk a little bit
about container networking.

00:22:00.430 --> 00:22:02.560
So for those running
containerized workloads

00:22:02.560 --> 00:22:04.630
on Google Cloud, I
want to talk about how

00:22:04.630 --> 00:22:07.780
we've enhanced the experience
and really made containers

00:22:07.780 --> 00:22:11.080
first class citizens with
regard to our Load Balancing

00:22:11.080 --> 00:22:12.850
offerings.

00:22:12.850 --> 00:22:15.500
So I'm really excited to talk
about Network Endpoint Groups.

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:17.940
So what Network
Endpoint Groups are,

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:20.920
they're a collection
of network endpoints.

00:22:20.920 --> 00:22:25.930
So fundamentally, this
maps to a port and IP pair.

00:22:25.930 --> 00:22:30.000
So as a precursor to
Network Endpoint Groups,

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:33.740
VMs can have multiple
ranges associated to them.

00:22:33.740 --> 00:22:35.325
So we use alias IP ranges.

00:22:35.325 --> 00:22:38.950
And in that sense, a VM can
host multiple containers

00:22:38.950 --> 00:22:44.590
with VPC routeable IP addresses,
along with serving ports.

00:22:44.590 --> 00:22:46.600
So now, with Network
Endpoint Groups,

00:22:46.600 --> 00:22:50.080
we can target these IPs
along with serving port

00:22:50.080 --> 00:22:52.420
as an endpoint, health
check and load balance

00:22:52.420 --> 00:22:54.370
accordingly and
much more accurately

00:22:54.370 --> 00:22:56.560
without Network Endpoint Groups.

00:22:56.560 --> 00:22:59.120
So the diagram on
the left here--

00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:01.120
I apologize, it's
a little choppy--

00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:04.900
shows a Kubernetes deployment
without Network Endpoint

00:23:04.900 --> 00:23:05.420
Groups.

00:23:05.420 --> 00:23:06.836
And one of the
things you will see

00:23:06.836 --> 00:23:10.660
is that the Load Balancer
targets nodes, not endpoints,

00:23:10.660 --> 00:23:12.070
not containers themselves.

00:23:12.070 --> 00:23:15.020
So what happens is traffic
arrives at the Load Balancer.

00:23:15.020 --> 00:23:17.070
We select a back end node.

00:23:17.070 --> 00:23:19.690
And then, with
Kubernetes Engine,

00:23:19.690 --> 00:23:23.110
we have queue proxy,
which basically configures

00:23:23.110 --> 00:23:26.140
a bunch of IP tables rules
almost as a secondary Load

00:23:26.140 --> 00:23:30.340
Balancer to distribute
traffic to the back end pods.

00:23:30.340 --> 00:23:33.160
So depending on how many
pods-- so let's assume

00:23:33.160 --> 00:23:36.280
you have an equal distribution
of pods across your nodes.

00:23:36.280 --> 00:23:38.740
Well, you have a 1
divided by n, which

00:23:38.740 --> 00:23:43.720
is the number of nodes,
chance that a node outside

00:23:43.720 --> 00:23:47.290
of the local node your
traffic arrives at

00:23:47.290 --> 00:23:49.720
will be selected for
this Load Balancer.

00:23:49.720 --> 00:23:52.210
So in this case, traffic
comes into the node.

00:23:52.210 --> 00:23:54.130
IP tables will do
some masquerading

00:23:54.130 --> 00:23:58.360
and send that traffic to a
pod located on another node.

00:23:58.360 --> 00:23:59.680
It also sorts NATs.

00:23:59.680 --> 00:24:01.000
So you have these extra hops.

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:05.470
Then the traffic returns
from the pod back

00:24:05.470 --> 00:24:08.140
to the original node, and
then back to the Load Balancer

00:24:08.140 --> 00:24:09.530
and then back to the client.

00:24:09.530 --> 00:24:10.780
So its fairly suboptimal.

00:24:10.780 --> 00:24:12.310
Now, you can turn
on a mode which

00:24:12.310 --> 00:24:15.370
is known as Local
Only, which will just

00:24:15.370 --> 00:24:18.340
target the pods
local to the node

00:24:18.340 --> 00:24:20.180
that the traffic arrives on.

00:24:20.180 --> 00:24:23.560
But even then, you're not going
to get an even distribution.

00:24:23.560 --> 00:24:26.530
On the right hand diagram, we
show Network Endpoint Groups.

00:24:26.530 --> 00:24:29.110
In this model, the load
balancer sends traffic

00:24:29.110 --> 00:24:33.080
directly to the port and IP
pairs for the containers.

00:24:33.080 --> 00:24:35.680
This is enabled with a
simple annotation, right?

00:24:35.680 --> 00:24:36.982
This was an alpha feature.

00:24:36.982 --> 00:24:38.440
But with one simple
annotation, you

00:24:38.440 --> 00:24:39.606
can eliminate a lot of hops.

00:24:39.606 --> 00:24:43.360
And I'm going to show a
quick demo on this later.

00:24:43.360 --> 00:24:46.880
The next bit I want to talk
about is multicluster ingress.

00:24:46.880 --> 00:24:49.460
So if you have an
application running

00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:51.665
on Kubernetes Engine
in different regions--

00:24:51.665 --> 00:24:53.570
so you have different
regional clusters

00:24:53.570 --> 00:24:55.790
setup but the same
application-- you

00:24:55.790 --> 00:24:58.520
can now deploy a
multicluster ingress.

00:24:58.520 --> 00:25:01.370
Which basically, you can
register your container

00:25:01.370 --> 00:25:03.770
clusters to and create
a unified ingress that

00:25:03.770 --> 00:25:07.250
can load balance client traffic
using all that global load

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:11.940
balancing magic to the
appropriate container cluster.

00:25:11.940 --> 00:25:17.210
So this is an enhancement to
our queue Control interface.

00:25:17.210 --> 00:25:20.690
So with Cube MCI, you can
create this new ingress

00:25:20.690 --> 00:25:24.340
that will map multiple clusters
to a single Load Balancer.

00:25:27.661 --> 00:25:28.160
All right.

00:25:28.160 --> 00:25:29.701
So here's the demo
environment again.

00:25:29.701 --> 00:25:32.237
I want to show Network
Endpoint Groups.

00:25:32.237 --> 00:25:33.070
And I'll prove this.

00:25:33.070 --> 00:25:34.861
There's no smoke and
mirrors going on here.

00:25:34.861 --> 00:25:39.880
So I have one cluster, which
is Cluster A. That is deployed

00:25:39.880 --> 00:25:41.500
with Network Endpoint Groups.

00:25:41.500 --> 00:25:44.070
So we have a simple
web application that's

00:25:44.070 --> 00:25:46.030
just returning its host name.

00:25:46.030 --> 00:25:47.500
We have load
generators, so we're

00:25:47.500 --> 00:25:49.440
going to blast a bunch
of traffic to it.

00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:53.860
And we're going to observe some
monitoring metrics on this.

00:25:53.860 --> 00:25:58.300
The other cluster is deployed
in the same zone, US-West 1A--

00:25:58.300 --> 00:26:00.760
same number of nodes,
same application,

00:26:00.760 --> 00:26:02.350
same load testing
characteristics.

00:26:02.350 --> 00:26:05.560
So we're sending 50
concurrent clients connecting

00:26:05.560 --> 00:26:08.770
into this environment.

00:26:08.770 --> 00:26:11.020
But this is not using
Network Endpoint Groups.

00:26:15.190 --> 00:26:15.690
All right.

00:26:15.690 --> 00:26:17.670
So let's jump back
into our demo.

00:26:23.280 --> 00:26:23.780
All right.

00:26:23.780 --> 00:26:25.220
So back in the Cloud
Console, I just

00:26:25.220 --> 00:26:27.219
want to give you a quick
tour of the environment

00:26:27.219 --> 00:26:29.160
so you can't call me out later.

00:26:29.160 --> 00:26:31.820
So here you can see
our two clusters.

00:26:31.820 --> 00:26:33.590
They both have three nodes.

00:26:33.590 --> 00:26:35.714
They're basically identical.

00:26:35.714 --> 00:26:37.880
As far as the deployments
we have in these clusters,

00:26:37.880 --> 00:26:40.040
you can see our Network
Endpoint Group demo

00:26:40.040 --> 00:26:46.250
app has five replicas in our
Cluster A, which is our Network

00:26:46.250 --> 00:26:47.900
Endpoint Group cluster.

00:26:47.900 --> 00:26:52.460
The non-NEG or, you know, the
traditional app is running

00:26:52.460 --> 00:26:56.040
in Cluster B.

00:26:56.040 --> 00:26:57.920
And then if we look
at the services,

00:26:57.920 --> 00:27:00.770
both of these clusters are,
again, running five pods.

00:27:00.770 --> 00:27:04.280
And then we have ingress of
the HTTP(S) Load Balancer.

00:27:04.280 --> 00:27:10.820
So if we jump over to the
HTTP(S) Load Balancer screen,

00:27:10.820 --> 00:27:13.360
you'll see that in our network
endpoint group cluster,

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:17.470
you notice that the back end
service is targeting a network

00:27:17.470 --> 00:27:18.140
endpoint group.

00:27:18.140 --> 00:27:20.090
So these are built zonally.

00:27:20.090 --> 00:27:22.710
So if we had a
regional cluster, you

00:27:22.710 --> 00:27:25.060
would see a network
endpoint group per zone.

00:27:25.060 --> 00:27:27.580
But again, these are
port and IP pairs.

00:27:27.580 --> 00:27:32.400
And if we jump back to
the non-NEG cluster,

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:34.240
you can see that
the Load Balancer

00:27:34.240 --> 00:27:37.620
is targeting the nodes, right?

00:27:37.620 --> 00:27:41.620
So you know, IP tables is
involved, masquerading packets.

00:27:41.620 --> 00:27:43.650
So if we look at
Stackdriver monitoring,

00:27:43.650 --> 00:27:45.525
there's a couple of
interesting observations.

00:27:45.525 --> 00:27:47.500
So I'm capturing or
charting two metrics.

00:27:47.500 --> 00:27:49.940
The first is
requests per second.

00:27:49.940 --> 00:27:53.250
And the other is
aggregate network traffic

00:27:53.250 --> 00:27:55.300
on the back end nodes.

00:27:55.300 --> 00:27:58.510
So even though the load
testing is the exact same,

00:27:58.510 --> 00:28:00.900
you'll notice we're
able to send about 10%

00:28:00.900 --> 00:28:04.240
more connections to the
Network Endpoint Group cluster.

00:28:04.240 --> 00:28:04.980
I think this is--

00:28:04.980 --> 00:28:07.860
don't take my word for
this-- but I believe this is

00:28:07.860 --> 00:28:11.460
because the transactions
are finishing faster

00:28:11.460 --> 00:28:13.650
so the performance--

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:16.050
the traffic generator is
able to pump more traffic

00:28:16.050 --> 00:28:19.000
to the Network
Endpoint Group cluster.

00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:20.230
This is key here, though.

00:28:20.230 --> 00:28:23.100
You'll notice that the
Network Endpoint Group

00:28:23.100 --> 00:28:26.400
cluster has about half
of the aggregate traffic

00:28:26.400 --> 00:28:29.910
as the non-NEG Network
Endpoint Group cluster.

00:28:29.910 --> 00:28:31.160
So this is substantial, right?

00:28:31.160 --> 00:28:33.649
This is all a factor of
eliminating those hops.

00:28:33.649 --> 00:28:35.190
But the other thing
is, like, imagine

00:28:35.190 --> 00:28:37.470
if you're running a
regional cluster where you

00:28:37.470 --> 00:28:39.097
have nodes in different zones.

00:28:39.097 --> 00:28:40.680
This could amount
to huge cost savings

00:28:40.680 --> 00:28:43.590
because you're not getting
charged zonal egress

00:28:43.590 --> 00:28:47.340
between the nodes, right?

00:28:47.340 --> 00:28:49.035
So pretty quick demo there.

00:28:49.035 --> 00:28:50.160
Hopefully that was helpful.

00:28:50.160 --> 00:28:51.618
I'll hand it back
over to Prajakta.

00:28:56.220 --> 00:28:59.010
PRAJAKTA JOSHI:
Great thanks, Mike.

00:28:59.010 --> 00:29:01.530
So what we did is
so far, we wanted

00:29:01.530 --> 00:29:04.480
to provide you a very
close look at Global Load

00:29:04.480 --> 00:29:06.990
Balancing and then our
Container Load Balancing.

00:29:06.990 --> 00:29:08.760
The next half of this
presentation we'll

00:29:08.760 --> 00:29:11.730
just quickly walk you
through all of the features.

00:29:11.730 --> 00:29:13.962
Many of these actually
have their own sessions.

00:29:13.962 --> 00:29:15.420
But the idea is
that you're exposed

00:29:15.420 --> 00:29:17.230
to a lot of these features.

00:29:17.230 --> 00:29:19.710
So the first one of those
is Internal Load Balancing

00:29:19.710 --> 00:29:22.320
because not every
service of yours needs

00:29:22.320 --> 00:29:23.640
to have a public-facing VIP.

00:29:23.640 --> 00:29:25.530
So that's the first one.

00:29:25.530 --> 00:29:27.930
You can see a very traditional
internal Load Balancer

00:29:27.930 --> 00:29:30.750
where you've got a
client in RFC 1918 space,

00:29:30.750 --> 00:29:35.100
Load Balancer VIP in RFC
1918 space, and a back end.

00:29:35.100 --> 00:29:37.920
This is also a traditional
3-tier app where the first tier

00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:39.542
is essentially public facing.

00:29:39.542 --> 00:29:41.000
And then the internal
load balancer

00:29:41.000 --> 00:29:43.230
is used for rest of the tiers.

00:29:43.230 --> 00:29:44.797
The most interesting
bit, actually,

00:29:44.797 --> 00:29:47.130
about the internal load
balancer is that there is really

00:29:47.130 --> 00:29:48.720
no internal load balancer.

00:29:48.720 --> 00:29:50.669
So between your clients
and your back ends,

00:29:50.669 --> 00:29:52.710
there is really no middle
proxy sitting and doing

00:29:52.710 --> 00:29:54.100
load balancing.

00:29:54.100 --> 00:29:57.300
We have Andromeda, which is our
network virtualization stack.

00:29:57.300 --> 00:30:00.690
And then that plants the client,
along with health information,

00:30:00.690 --> 00:30:03.960
so that the client itself can
go pick the optimal back end.

00:30:03.960 --> 00:30:05.820
So that way there's
no choke point

00:30:05.820 --> 00:30:09.570
in your network with
internal load balancer.

00:30:09.570 --> 00:30:11.090
The other thing,
you know, when you

00:30:11.090 --> 00:30:13.114
start with private
services, the next set

00:30:13.114 --> 00:30:14.780
of things that you're
going to go tackle

00:30:14.780 --> 00:30:17.400
is essentially securing
the edge because all

00:30:17.400 --> 00:30:19.880
of your internet0facing services
are going to get DDoSed.

00:30:19.880 --> 00:30:22.550
And these would be either
infrastructure DDoS attacks,

00:30:22.550 --> 00:30:25.250
or these would be even
application level attacks.

00:30:25.250 --> 00:30:26.840
So we have a bunch.

00:30:26.840 --> 00:30:29.360
Like, when you actually
secure your network,

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:32.840
you want to do it all the way
from VPC to your edge network.

00:30:32.840 --> 00:30:36.110
And we have several great
sessions on each of these.

00:30:36.110 --> 00:30:38.640
One thing I want to point
out is one best practice

00:30:38.640 --> 00:30:40.820
is to put TLS wherever you can.

00:30:40.820 --> 00:30:45.360
We don't charge extra for TLS,
so that should be your default.

00:30:45.360 --> 00:30:48.950
The second thing is, if you have
this need for multiple certs,

00:30:48.950 --> 00:30:51.800
we have a feature that lets you
put multiple certs on a given

00:30:51.800 --> 00:30:53.270
load balancer.

00:30:53.270 --> 00:30:56.090
And then many of you
complained about the toil

00:30:56.090 --> 00:30:57.560
of procuring certs.

00:30:57.560 --> 00:31:00.350
So we do have managed
certs which are in alpha.

00:31:00.350 --> 00:31:04.390
They will be available in
beta this year at NEXT.

00:31:04.390 --> 00:31:07.252
[APPLAUSE]

00:31:10.120 --> 00:31:14.410
The other thing is, many of you
said, can I turn off TLS 1.0

00:31:14.410 --> 00:31:17.120
because I need to pass
my PCI compliance test.

00:31:17.120 --> 00:31:20.680
So we also have custom SSL
policies available for you.

00:31:20.680 --> 00:31:23.410
Either you can outsource-- you
can select what kind of policy

00:31:23.410 --> 00:31:25.900
you want, and you can
outsource management to Google.

00:31:25.900 --> 00:31:28.060
Or then you can go create
your own custom ones

00:31:28.060 --> 00:31:30.910
if your IT department
has certain regulations

00:31:30.910 --> 00:31:32.414
around those.

00:31:32.414 --> 00:31:34.330
The other feature that
I also want to call out

00:31:34.330 --> 00:31:35.950
is as a best
practice, you should

00:31:35.950 --> 00:31:39.550
be turning on Global LB with
TLS, all of the features we've

00:31:39.550 --> 00:31:41.060
talked about, Cloud Armor.

00:31:41.060 --> 00:31:43.930
And then if you are
controlling access

00:31:43.930 --> 00:31:46.210
based on user identities,
you would also

00:31:46.210 --> 00:31:48.040
layer in Identity-Aware Proxy.

00:31:48.040 --> 00:31:50.510
So all of these work
together pretty well.

00:31:50.510 --> 00:31:53.110
And then also, if
Cloud Armor says

00:31:53.110 --> 00:31:57.040
drop traffic and Identity-Aware
Proxy says allow traffic,

00:31:57.040 --> 00:31:58.134
it's a combined thing.

00:31:58.134 --> 00:31:59.800
So it will essentially
drop the traffic.

00:31:59.800 --> 00:32:04.120
So they all work pretty
nicely along with each other.

00:32:04.120 --> 00:32:05.900
What I wanted to do now is--

00:32:05.900 --> 00:32:07.800
and of course, we not
going to deep dive

00:32:07.800 --> 00:32:09.174
into the features
of Cloud Armor.

00:32:09.174 --> 00:32:11.010
We had several
sessions on those.

00:32:11.010 --> 00:32:12.940
It goes all the way
from IP blacklist,

00:32:12.940 --> 00:32:16.030
whitelist to sophisticated
Layer-7 type defense.

00:32:16.030 --> 00:32:19.240
But we wanted to do a
quick demo with Mike.

00:32:19.240 --> 00:32:20.710
MIKE COLUMBUS: All right.

00:32:20.710 --> 00:32:21.690
Back again.

00:32:21.690 --> 00:32:23.810
So today for this
session, we're going

00:32:23.810 --> 00:32:26.920
to be tying some security
policy to our existing demo

00:32:26.920 --> 00:32:29.450
that we just went through.

00:32:29.450 --> 00:32:33.640
So I pinned network
security here.

00:32:33.640 --> 00:32:36.880
So we're going to be configuring
an SSL policy as well

00:32:36.880 --> 00:32:39.540
as a Cloud Armor policy.

00:32:39.540 --> 00:32:43.140
And I'll show you how that gets
attached to your Load Balancers

00:32:43.140 --> 00:32:46.870
to provide additional
protection.

00:32:46.870 --> 00:32:48.610
So we'll start with Cloud Armor.

00:32:48.610 --> 00:32:51.670
You can see I have a Cloud
Armor Security Policy here.

00:32:51.670 --> 00:32:55.460
This is a simple policy, but you
can always add to these later.

00:32:55.460 --> 00:32:58.900
So this is titled, Allow
Conference Net Block.

00:32:58.900 --> 00:33:03.880
So the idea here is that this
is an IP Whitelist Blacklist

00:33:03.880 --> 00:33:05.290
policy.

00:33:05.290 --> 00:33:08.660
So the rule set
is fairly simple.

00:33:08.660 --> 00:33:10.930
We're going to allow any--

00:33:10.930 --> 00:33:13.810
so I got the Moscone
network ranges.

00:33:13.810 --> 00:33:15.256
Hopefully these are accurate.

00:33:15.256 --> 00:33:16.880
Hopefully you get
online to test these.

00:33:16.880 --> 00:33:19.300
So basically, we're
going to allow this.

00:33:19.300 --> 00:33:21.160
So you can see some
IPv4 ranges in there,

00:33:21.160 --> 00:33:23.284
along with an IPv6 range.

00:33:23.284 --> 00:33:25.450
And then we're going to
deny everything else, right?

00:33:25.450 --> 00:33:27.670
So if you have an
application that, you know,

00:33:27.670 --> 00:33:30.490
you want to make sure you're
explicit as far as which

00:33:30.490 --> 00:33:31.932
IP addresses are
allowed to access

00:33:31.932 --> 00:33:33.640
that, this allows you
to obviously filter

00:33:33.640 --> 00:33:34.544
that at the edge.

00:33:34.544 --> 00:33:36.460
And don't worry about
it on your back end VMs.

00:33:36.460 --> 00:33:38.210
We take care of it.

00:33:38.210 --> 00:33:41.800
So once the rule's created, you
know, we've added our rule set,

00:33:41.800 --> 00:33:44.380
which is fairly simple here
with the deny at the end.

00:33:44.380 --> 00:33:47.570
The order that the rules are
evaluated is from low to high.

00:33:47.570 --> 00:33:48.970
So low is the first.

00:33:48.970 --> 00:33:51.370
So we created a
priority 100 rule here

00:33:51.370 --> 00:33:53.440
so we have some room to grow.

00:33:53.440 --> 00:33:55.210
We attached this
policy to targets.

00:33:55.210 --> 00:33:57.240
So you can attach this
policy to a single target

00:33:57.240 --> 00:33:58.550
or multiple targets.

00:33:58.550 --> 00:34:01.840
I already attached this policy
to our secure back end service.

00:34:01.840 --> 00:34:08.770
So the idea is if you are
on the local network here,

00:34:08.770 --> 00:34:12.461
you should have
access to www.https,

00:34:12.461 --> 00:34:18.429
www.gcpnetworking.com/secure/,
and make sure there's a tailing

00:34:18.429 --> 00:34:20.993
slash there too for access.

00:34:20.993 --> 00:34:22.909
If you're not, you're
not going to get access.

00:34:22.909 --> 00:34:26.199
So one way I can prove this is
I'm on the network right now.

00:34:26.199 --> 00:34:28.780
Let's open up a new tab and
connect to our Edge Security

00:34:28.780 --> 00:34:29.462
demo.

00:34:29.462 --> 00:34:30.670
And you can see we got there.

00:34:30.670 --> 00:34:32.604
The page looks a
little bit different.

00:34:32.604 --> 00:34:35.270
It's about the extent of
the customizations I did.

00:34:35.270 --> 00:34:38.199
But again, this is connecting
into the other back end

00:34:38.199 --> 00:34:39.699
service.

00:34:39.699 --> 00:34:43.510
One way to test that
this doesn't work

00:34:43.510 --> 00:34:45.940
is to use our Cloud Shell.

00:34:45.940 --> 00:34:49.780
So Cloud Shell is
basically a jump host

00:34:49.780 --> 00:34:51.800
pre-installed with our
software development kit.

00:34:51.800 --> 00:34:54.341
So basically, it's an easy way
to interface with your project

00:34:54.341 --> 00:34:56.211
programmatically.

00:34:56.211 --> 00:34:56.960
It's a Linux host.

00:34:56.960 --> 00:35:04.230
So we'll just cURL to the
secured back end service.

00:35:04.230 --> 00:35:07.330
And who thinks I'm going
to be able to connect?

00:35:07.330 --> 00:35:07.830
I hope not.

00:35:14.970 --> 00:35:16.420
And you can see I got a 403.

00:35:16.420 --> 00:35:18.320
So the actual error
response is configurable.

00:35:18.320 --> 00:35:22.840
You can send a 403, a
404, Not Found, or a 502.

00:35:22.840 --> 00:35:26.770
So in this rule, I
had configured a 403.

00:35:26.770 --> 00:35:28.670
So that's Cloud Armor.

00:35:28.670 --> 00:35:30.440
A very simple example
of Cloud Armor.

00:35:30.440 --> 00:35:32.820
And the key takeaway here
is that it's attached

00:35:32.820 --> 00:35:33.980
to your back end service.

00:35:33.980 --> 00:35:37.075
So if we wanted to
connect to, you know,

00:35:37.075 --> 00:35:43.902
the kind of the naked URL
here, it would work just fine.

00:35:43.902 --> 00:35:46.360
It's because we don't have the
policy tied to that back end

00:35:46.360 --> 00:35:47.670
service.

00:35:47.670 --> 00:35:49.590
SSL policies, as
Prajakta mentioned,

00:35:49.590 --> 00:35:52.130
is a powerful tool to
enforce compliance.

00:35:52.130 --> 00:35:54.420
So say if we wanted to
follow PCI guidance and say,

00:35:54.420 --> 00:35:56.253
all right, I only want
my clients to connect

00:35:56.253 --> 00:36:00.359
into my environment with
a minimum of TLS1.2.

00:36:00.359 --> 00:36:01.900
It's kind of like
Cloud Armor, right?

00:36:01.900 --> 00:36:03.970
You create the policy,
but in this case,

00:36:03.970 --> 00:36:06.690
you attach it to the target
proxy and the global forwarding

00:36:06.690 --> 00:36:08.160
rules.

00:36:08.160 --> 00:36:10.870
So we created a policy that
specifies minimum TLS version

00:36:10.870 --> 00:36:14.220
is 1.2 with the
restricted profile.

00:36:14.220 --> 00:36:17.370
But, you know, you can
create custom profiles

00:36:17.370 --> 00:36:20.910
and explicitly allow
or disallow ciphers.

00:36:20.910 --> 00:36:24.615
We also have a modern profile to
support a wide set of clients.

00:36:24.615 --> 00:36:26.490
Then we also have a
compatible profile, which

00:36:26.490 --> 00:36:27.720
is a little bit more loose.

00:36:27.720 --> 00:36:29.410
Or you can create your own.

00:36:29.410 --> 00:36:33.930
So for this, we created a
minimum TLS version of 1.2

00:36:33.930 --> 00:36:37.050
and we attached this
to the Load Balancer.

00:36:37.050 --> 00:36:45.360
So now, if we do a cURL command
but specify the TLS version,

00:36:45.360 --> 00:36:47.780
so let's try to connect
with version 1.0.

00:36:47.780 --> 00:36:50.480
You see we get an SSL
protocol version error.

00:36:50.480 --> 00:36:54.240
If we try 1.2,
same error, right?

00:36:54.240 --> 00:36:56.000
We only want to support 1.3.

00:36:56.000 --> 00:36:57.071
So if we do 1.--

00:36:57.071 --> 00:36:57.570
I'm sorry.

00:36:57.570 --> 00:36:59.410
We only support 1.2 or above.

00:36:59.410 --> 00:37:00.270
So 1.2.

00:37:00.270 --> 00:37:01.770
We specify that for
the TLS version.

00:37:01.770 --> 00:37:02.870
Works no problem.

00:37:02.870 --> 00:37:03.777
All right.

00:37:03.777 --> 00:37:05.610
So with that, I'll hand
it back to Prajakta.

00:37:13.890 --> 00:37:16.720
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: So again, the
next set of features are--

00:37:16.720 --> 00:37:19.530
I'm going to put some
of the work on you

00:37:19.530 --> 00:37:21.497
in that, after the session,
do go and check out

00:37:21.497 --> 00:37:23.580
all of these features
because they do bring really

00:37:23.580 --> 00:37:26.540
good benefits to your services.

00:37:26.540 --> 00:37:28.890
The main thing, after you've
done putting your services

00:37:28.890 --> 00:37:31.330
in place and then you've
secured the services,

00:37:31.330 --> 00:37:33.330
the next thing you
want to do is optimize.

00:37:33.330 --> 00:37:36.180
And I think the first thing that
people talk about optimizing

00:37:36.180 --> 00:37:37.780
is for latency.

00:37:37.780 --> 00:37:39.970
So the first, we already
spoke about the Global LB,

00:37:39.970 --> 00:37:42.540
we spoke about the two
tiered architecture.

00:37:42.540 --> 00:37:44.790
But the main takeaway
is your SSL termination

00:37:44.790 --> 00:37:46.000
happens at the edge.

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:49.050
And that saves a lot
in terms of latency.

00:37:49.050 --> 00:37:52.950
The second thing is that once
your first handshake is done,

00:37:52.950 --> 00:37:56.189
then essentially after that,
the connections are maintained.

00:37:56.189 --> 00:37:58.230
And so your next handshake,
like you're not truly

00:37:58.230 --> 00:38:00.390
doing a handshake all
the way to the end.

00:38:00.390 --> 00:38:02.190
But for all of your
subsequent connections,

00:38:02.190 --> 00:38:04.390
you're essentially
saving on round trips.

00:38:04.390 --> 00:38:07.470
So you got this just by putting
the Global Load Balancer.

00:38:07.470 --> 00:38:10.840
One of the really good
ways to just shave down

00:38:10.840 --> 00:38:13.980
more of your latency is if
your content is cacheable,

00:38:13.980 --> 00:38:15.440
you would go down on CDN.

00:38:15.440 --> 00:38:18.450
So we have Cloud CDN, which
is our native offering.

00:38:18.450 --> 00:38:20.470
And you can turn it on
with a single checkbox.

00:38:20.470 --> 00:38:22.260
So if you have a
Load Balancer, you

00:38:22.260 --> 00:38:24.820
can just turn on a checkbox,
as you can see there.

00:38:24.820 --> 00:38:27.390
And then you can enable it.

00:38:27.390 --> 00:38:29.370
There is also a public
report, which is

00:38:29.370 --> 00:38:36.570
www.cedexis.com/google-reports/,
were Cedexis measured Cloud CDN

00:38:36.570 --> 00:38:37.710
against other providers.

00:38:37.710 --> 00:38:39.600
And then you can see
that it does really

00:38:39.600 --> 00:38:41.850
well in terms of low
latency, high throughput,

00:38:41.850 --> 00:38:44.880
and availability.

00:38:44.880 --> 00:38:46.590
The other thing is
something that we've

00:38:46.590 --> 00:38:48.506
been thinking about at
Google for a long time,

00:38:48.506 --> 00:38:50.980
like, how do you go and
make the Web faster?

00:38:50.980 --> 00:38:53.050
And some of the key points
I wanted to call out.

00:38:53.050 --> 00:38:54.591
So we spoke about
the infrastructure,

00:38:54.591 --> 00:38:56.670
we spoke about the Global LB.

00:38:56.670 --> 00:38:59.440
There are two additional things
that I wanted to look at.

00:38:59.440 --> 00:39:03.030
One is HTTP2, which essentially
goes and improves HTTP.

00:39:03.030 --> 00:39:06.960
And then there is QUIC, which
is a protocol which is over UDP.

00:39:06.960 --> 00:39:11.970
And in fact, if you're using a
Chrome browser during the day,

00:39:11.970 --> 00:39:13.620
you've already used QUIC.

00:39:13.620 --> 00:39:15.960
And, you know, it does
some really cool things.

00:39:15.960 --> 00:39:19.140
So if you think of why
your latency is there,

00:39:19.140 --> 00:39:21.240
it's because of things
like head of line blocking.

00:39:21.240 --> 00:39:24.150
So there's a bunch of features
built into QUIC, which actually

00:39:24.150 --> 00:39:27.030
stands for Quick UDP Internet
Connections, that help

00:39:27.030 --> 00:39:28.710
you bring the latency down.

00:39:28.710 --> 00:39:31.800
I'm going to show you how
you would configure it.

00:39:31.800 --> 00:39:34.639
A lot of times, people
will say we support HTTP2.

00:39:34.639 --> 00:39:36.930
The first thing you need to
see is from where to where.

00:39:36.930 --> 00:39:40.920
So we support HTTP2 from
client to the Load Balancer,

00:39:40.920 --> 00:39:42.660
and then Load Balancer
to the back end.

00:39:42.660 --> 00:39:44.910
And you would need
that to run gRPC.

00:39:44.910 --> 00:39:46.050
Same way with QUIC.

00:39:46.050 --> 00:39:48.600
We support from client
to the Load Balancer.

00:39:48.600 --> 00:39:50.670
And we're the first
Cloud to actually support

00:39:50.670 --> 00:39:54.410
that, the first major
public Cloud to do that.

00:39:54.410 --> 00:39:56.900
And what it really
gives you is this.

00:39:56.900 --> 00:39:59.420
So when we turned
on QUIC for our CDN,

00:39:59.420 --> 00:40:01.377
you can see the sharp
jump in throughput

00:40:01.377 --> 00:40:02.960
because you've brought
down latencies.

00:40:02.960 --> 00:40:05.030
You can essentially
have more connections.

00:40:05.030 --> 00:40:09.080
And then what you see on the
right is how did it bring down

00:40:09.080 --> 00:40:09.704
this latency?

00:40:09.704 --> 00:40:11.120
And you can see
that the handshake

00:40:11.120 --> 00:40:14.550
looks much smaller when you
have QUIC versus without QUIC.

00:40:14.550 --> 00:40:16.370
So we have a blog
post that we posted

00:40:16.370 --> 00:40:18.800
which describes all of
these details as well,

00:40:18.800 --> 00:40:21.246
but that is one of the features
you should be looking at

00:40:21.246 --> 00:40:22.370
to bring down your latency.

00:40:25.312 --> 00:40:27.520
Now, these are some of the
features that Mike already

00:40:27.520 --> 00:40:30.340
spoke about, which
is network tiers.

00:40:30.340 --> 00:40:33.820
So when your traffic
egresses out of Google Cloud,

00:40:33.820 --> 00:40:36.850
you're paying the outbound
data transfer costs.

00:40:36.850 --> 00:40:39.130
You should by default
use the Premium Tier

00:40:39.130 --> 00:40:41.590
because that makes your return
traffic over Google's Global

00:40:41.590 --> 00:40:42.460
Network.

00:40:42.460 --> 00:40:44.770
But you may have services
which, for instance, are

00:40:44.770 --> 00:40:47.200
a free tier for you, or
you may have services

00:40:47.200 --> 00:40:48.590
that are not mission critical.

00:40:48.590 --> 00:40:50.830
And if you want to bring
down the cost there,

00:40:50.830 --> 00:40:54.580
you can actually go configure
the Load Balancer to use, just

00:40:54.580 --> 00:40:56.830
like other public
Clouds, just standard ISP

00:40:56.830 --> 00:40:58.810
transit, which is
not Google's network,

00:40:58.810 --> 00:41:00.580
to go back to your user.

00:41:00.580 --> 00:41:03.040
And so the main
thing we want to do

00:41:03.040 --> 00:41:06.220
is that we wanted you to
have choice per workload.

00:41:06.220 --> 00:41:08.920
So you can actually have this
configuration per Load Balancer

00:41:08.920 --> 00:41:09.804
if you want to.

00:41:09.804 --> 00:41:12.220
Or, if you just want to put
it at the project level, which

00:41:12.220 --> 00:41:14.910
is our thing which holds
all of the load balancers,

00:41:14.910 --> 00:41:15.910
you can do that as well.

00:41:18.650 --> 00:41:21.190
This one is, again, something
that Mike spoke about,

00:41:21.190 --> 00:41:25.210
which is when you configure
your application instances,

00:41:25.210 --> 00:41:28.210
we have a feature called user
defined request headers which

00:41:28.210 --> 00:41:31.700
lets you put things such as
client geolocation, or RTT,

00:41:31.700 --> 00:41:35.170
or TLS information, and then
send it to your back end.

00:41:35.170 --> 00:41:37.210
So then your back
ends can use that.

00:41:37.210 --> 00:41:39.280
And they can use it in
their application logic

00:41:39.280 --> 00:41:41.656
to make optimized decisions.

00:41:44.530 --> 00:41:47.239
So, Mike, recap
everything we spoke about.

00:41:47.239 --> 00:41:48.280
MIKE COLUMBUS: All right.

00:41:48.280 --> 00:41:50.330
So we tried to--

00:41:50.330 --> 00:41:52.630
as she walks off the stage.

00:41:52.630 --> 00:41:54.610
[LAUGHTER]

00:41:54.610 --> 00:41:55.110
All right.

00:41:55.110 --> 00:41:57.790
Some of these might actually
be new, but just kind

00:41:57.790 --> 00:41:59.030
of a cheat sheet, right?

00:41:59.030 --> 00:42:01.330
So the first thing
in general, right?

00:42:01.330 --> 00:42:02.830
Choose the optimal
Load Balancer.

00:42:02.830 --> 00:42:05.770
We have a few
different solutions.

00:42:05.770 --> 00:42:08.380
And there's not one
size fits all, right?

00:42:08.380 --> 00:42:13.690
So factors you want to consider,
do I need IPv6 support, right?

00:42:13.690 --> 00:42:15.280
Do I need TLS offload?

00:42:15.280 --> 00:42:17.620
Is it an internal versus
external application?

00:42:17.620 --> 00:42:19.990
Regional versus global?

00:42:19.990 --> 00:42:22.450
Do your back ends need
true client IP, right?

00:42:22.450 --> 00:42:24.880
These are going to guide you
to the right Load Balancer.

00:42:24.880 --> 00:42:27.421
Make sure you don't just start
deploying without knowing what

00:42:27.421 --> 00:42:29.372
you're getting yourself into.

00:42:29.372 --> 00:42:31.330
The other thing is optimize
for latency, right?

00:42:31.330 --> 00:42:33.480
So we have 20 regions.

00:42:33.480 --> 00:42:36.650
Deploy your workloads in the
region closest to your users.

00:42:36.650 --> 00:42:38.950
And if you're using a
Global Load Balancer,

00:42:38.950 --> 00:42:43.617
deploy to multiple regions
closest to your users.

00:42:43.617 --> 00:42:44.950
And use modern protocols, right?

00:42:44.950 --> 00:42:46.554
Like QUIC, HTTP2, right?

00:42:46.554 --> 00:42:48.220
All these things are
great to, you know,

00:42:48.220 --> 00:42:51.610
improve performance and also
help in lossy environments

00:42:51.610 --> 00:42:54.940
where the connections
might not be great.

00:42:54.940 --> 00:42:55.950
Secure your service.

00:42:55.950 --> 00:42:57.366
So there's a few
bits here, right?

00:42:57.366 --> 00:42:59.320
As Prajakta mentioned,
use TLS everywhere.

00:42:59.320 --> 00:43:00.790
Standardize on that.

00:43:00.790 --> 00:43:04.090
Use the VPC firewall to
augment your security policies.

00:43:04.090 --> 00:43:07.810
So health checks come
from known ranges.

00:43:07.810 --> 00:43:10.210
Any of our Proxy Load
Balancers, traffic

00:43:10.210 --> 00:43:11.970
comes from known ranges.

00:43:11.970 --> 00:43:13.000
Lock that down.

00:43:13.000 --> 00:43:14.509
These things are predictable.

00:43:14.509 --> 00:43:16.300
Secure at the edge with
Cloud Armor, right?

00:43:16.300 --> 00:43:18.100
Make sure you're using
that functionality

00:43:18.100 --> 00:43:22.390
to eliminate additional
security measures

00:43:22.390 --> 00:43:25.060
have a defense in depth strategy
and offload some of that

00:43:25.060 --> 00:43:26.320
to our Edge.

00:43:26.320 --> 00:43:27.170
The other thing is--

00:43:27.170 --> 00:43:29.710
and this is a common
one-- so your VMs do not

00:43:29.710 --> 00:43:32.830
need public IP addresses in any
of our Load Balancer solutions,

00:43:32.830 --> 00:43:34.150
right?

00:43:34.150 --> 00:43:36.820
Even though, you know,
that GFE IP, if you recall,

00:43:36.820 --> 00:43:39.440
when it connected back
into the back end,

00:43:39.440 --> 00:43:41.407
lock your firewall down
to that range, right?

00:43:41.407 --> 00:43:43.990
There's no need to expose your
back ends ends with a public IP

00:43:43.990 --> 00:43:46.230
address.

00:43:46.230 --> 00:43:47.920
Optimize for auto
scaling, right?

00:43:47.920 --> 00:43:49.660
Know the profile of
your application.

00:43:49.660 --> 00:43:51.050
Know how you want auto scale.

00:43:51.050 --> 00:43:52.930
If you have a
dual-headed service,

00:43:52.930 --> 00:43:54.430
if you have an
instance group that's

00:43:54.430 --> 00:43:56.470
a back end of two
load balancers,

00:43:56.470 --> 00:43:58.990
really think through how you
want to scale that, right?

00:43:58.990 --> 00:44:00.550
Do you want to use
a custom metric?

00:44:00.550 --> 00:44:04.575
Because the load balancers
will scale independently.

00:44:04.575 --> 00:44:06.450
Understand and optimize
health checks, right?

00:44:06.450 --> 00:44:10.000
Try to health check as close
to the application as you can.

00:44:10.000 --> 00:44:14.860
And, you know, as I
mentioned earlier,

00:44:14.860 --> 00:44:18.175
it's a less expensive operation
to remove a VM from a pool back

00:44:18.175 --> 00:44:21.610
end services and then
use auto healer to reboot

00:44:21.610 --> 00:44:23.460
that VM should it
become unresponsive,

00:44:23.460 --> 00:44:25.559
but relax those
timers a little bit.

00:44:25.559 --> 00:44:27.850
The other thing I'll say here
is that our health checks

00:44:27.850 --> 00:44:30.010
can be quite chatty.

00:44:30.010 --> 00:44:33.625
So if you're doing logging
on your back end VMs,

00:44:33.625 --> 00:44:35.125
make sure you're
aware of our ranges

00:44:35.125 --> 00:44:37.960
so you can treat them
accordingly on your logs.

00:44:37.960 --> 00:44:39.670
For the ILB or Network
Load, Balancer,

00:44:39.670 --> 00:44:40.960
there's UDP considerations.

00:44:40.960 --> 00:44:45.012
So these load
balancers for UDP--

00:44:45.012 --> 00:44:47.470
so if you think you're going
to get fragmented UDP packets,

00:44:47.470 --> 00:44:49.150
be careful with 5-tuple hashing.

00:44:49.150 --> 00:44:52.180
The reason is you can lose the
destination port as packets

00:44:52.180 --> 00:44:54.920
arrive on or load balancer and
those packets will be dropped.

00:44:54.920 --> 00:44:57.378
So you might be better off
using a different load balancing

00:44:57.378 --> 00:44:58.000
algorithm.

00:44:58.000 --> 00:45:00.790
The other thing is with UDP--
this is a common thing I see--

00:45:00.790 --> 00:45:04.840
make sure you're listening
on the actual VIP.

00:45:04.840 --> 00:45:07.300
So what happens is if you
put-- with the Network Load

00:45:07.300 --> 00:45:10.120
Balancer, the ILB, the
VIPs are known by the VMs.

00:45:10.120 --> 00:45:11.800
They get installed
as a Linux route.

00:45:11.800 --> 00:45:13.780
You want to listen on
that VIP on your back end

00:45:13.780 --> 00:45:16.200
VMs for UDP traffic.

00:45:16.200 --> 00:45:19.420
Use 5-tuple hashing
for the best entropy.

00:45:19.420 --> 00:45:22.830
A lot of times, people will load
test the Network Load Balancer,

00:45:22.830 --> 00:45:26.050
the ILB, and they'll send
the same source, dest port IP

00:45:26.050 --> 00:45:26.720
and protocol.

00:45:26.720 --> 00:45:29.136
And they're like, why isn't
this stuff getting distributed

00:45:29.136 --> 00:45:30.580
across my back ends?

00:45:30.580 --> 00:45:32.980
You know, you wouldn't want
to throw a variety of traffic

00:45:32.980 --> 00:45:34.750
at it just to see
traffic get balanced

00:45:34.750 --> 00:45:36.250
because it's using hashing.

00:45:36.250 --> 00:45:39.250
Use 2 or 3-tuple hashing
for session affinity.

00:45:39.250 --> 00:45:41.530
For the Global Load
Balancers, secure at the Edge.

00:45:41.530 --> 00:45:43.570
Use Capacity Scaler
for A/B deployments.

00:45:43.570 --> 00:45:45.736
It's a very powerful tool
where you could, you know,

00:45:45.736 --> 00:45:47.680
set capacity at
additional measure

00:45:47.680 --> 00:45:51.200
to start directing
traffic to new builds.

00:45:51.200 --> 00:45:53.170
Set the appropriate
connection time out.

00:45:53.170 --> 00:45:57.564
Use cookie or IP based affinity
for session of persistence.

00:45:57.564 --> 00:45:59.480
So with that, I'll have
Prajakta take us home.

00:46:01.058 --> 00:46:03.990
PRAJAKTA JOSHI: Thanks, Mike.

00:46:03.990 --> 00:46:07.800
So you know, we spoke about
everything that exists today.

00:46:07.800 --> 00:46:12.580
And the next question is,
what's next in load balancing?

00:46:12.580 --> 00:46:16.440
And we had an entire session
on it just prior to this one.

00:46:16.440 --> 00:46:19.410
But if you attended any of the
sessions in this conference,

00:46:19.410 --> 00:46:20.790
there were two main trends.

00:46:20.790 --> 00:46:23.280
One was monoliths
to microservices.

00:46:23.280 --> 00:46:25.740
And then the second
one is almost everybody

00:46:25.740 --> 00:46:28.290
has at least an on-prem
deployment in Google Cloud,

00:46:28.290 --> 00:46:31.830
or Google Cloud and another
cloud, or maybe all three.

00:46:31.830 --> 00:46:35.520
And delivering services
that span this,

00:46:35.520 --> 00:46:37.530
or even taking your
monolith, when you chop it

00:46:37.530 --> 00:46:41.800
to micro services, sure, you get
agility and so on and so forth.

00:46:41.800 --> 00:46:44.370
But it's still really hard
to deliver those services.

00:46:44.370 --> 00:46:47.040
And so Cloud Services
Platform, which

00:46:47.040 --> 00:46:49.590
was an announcement in
our keynote yesterday,

00:46:49.590 --> 00:46:51.450
is attempting to solve
those problems, where

00:46:51.450 --> 00:46:53.430
instead of giving
you point products,

00:46:53.430 --> 00:46:56.010
we're putting them all
together into a platform

00:46:56.010 --> 00:46:57.840
so you can actually
go and then build

00:46:57.840 --> 00:47:00.630
these hybrid or
multi-cloud services.

00:47:00.630 --> 00:47:02.850
I wanted to add two new
bits that we announced

00:47:02.850 --> 00:47:03.960
in the previous session.

00:47:03.960 --> 00:47:06.240
One is the Traffic
Director and then

00:47:06.240 --> 00:47:09.750
how it operates with Envoy,
which is a service proxy.

00:47:09.750 --> 00:47:11.820
I'm not going to go
into the details of it.

00:47:11.820 --> 00:47:15.780
But the simple thing I wanted
to show is this is a service.

00:47:15.780 --> 00:47:17.970
You brought in the
notion of a service mesh.

00:47:17.970 --> 00:47:20.760
So that's when a lot of this
load balancing is headed to.

00:47:20.760 --> 00:47:23.310
Where just like in the old
days, you took hardware boxes

00:47:23.310 --> 00:47:25.560
and you separated the
control and the data plane,

00:47:25.560 --> 00:47:27.490
you're doing exactly to
the same to services,

00:47:27.490 --> 00:47:33.091
where your data plane
is the service proxy

00:47:33.091 --> 00:47:35.340
and then your control plane
is your service management

00:47:35.340 --> 00:47:36.850
infrastructure.

00:47:36.850 --> 00:47:39.735
One of the most popular service
proxies that we see in use

00:47:39.735 --> 00:47:40.830
is Envoy.

00:47:40.830 --> 00:47:43.030
And our previous session
was centered around that.

00:47:43.030 --> 00:47:46.290
So if you get a chance, do
look at the Envoy proxy.

00:47:46.290 --> 00:47:49.780
And we like it because, first
of all, it's high performance.

00:47:49.780 --> 00:47:51.570
It provides a bunch
of features that would

00:47:51.570 --> 00:47:53.430
be required for your services.

00:47:53.430 --> 00:47:56.580
And then the third thing is
that also, it is very modern.

00:47:56.580 --> 00:48:00.280
So it's supporting things
like HTTP2 from the get go.

00:48:00.280 --> 00:48:02.580
So the moment you
can put a proxy

00:48:02.580 --> 00:48:04.740
next to your existing
service or application

00:48:04.740 --> 00:48:06.960
logic, the way
you build services

00:48:06.960 --> 00:48:11.490
can be fairly uniform because
now you can do it the same way.

00:48:11.490 --> 00:48:12.990
This is the
announcement that I want

00:48:12.990 --> 00:48:15.900
you to go and look at later,
which is Traffic Director.

00:48:15.900 --> 00:48:18.750
So what exactly does
Traffic Director do?

00:48:18.750 --> 00:48:20.880
Let me show you a picture here.

00:48:20.880 --> 00:48:23.640
You're so used to global load
balancing with our global load

00:48:23.640 --> 00:48:24.210
balancer.

00:48:24.210 --> 00:48:27.120
We spent a big chunk of
time talking about that.

00:48:27.120 --> 00:48:30.810
We want to bring that same
capability to microservices.

00:48:30.810 --> 00:48:33.370
And so what you can see here
is there's a shopping cart app.

00:48:33.370 --> 00:48:35.370
You've got the front end,
the shopping cart app,

00:48:35.370 --> 00:48:36.450
and the payment.

00:48:36.450 --> 00:48:38.580
But your instances
for your microservices

00:48:38.580 --> 00:48:40.420
are in two different regions.

00:48:40.420 --> 00:48:43.770
So one is in US Central, so
our data centers are in Iowa.

00:48:43.770 --> 00:48:46.504
And then the other one
is actually in Singapore.

00:48:46.504 --> 00:48:49.170
And what this means is that it's
not that they are two services.

00:48:49.170 --> 00:48:52.800
They're literally one service
with instances in both regions.

00:48:52.800 --> 00:48:54.840
And then the Traffic
Director is the one

00:48:54.840 --> 00:48:57.240
that's managing those
proxies that you see sitting

00:48:57.240 --> 00:48:58.860
on each of those instances.

00:48:58.860 --> 00:49:00.840
And that's the one that's
feeding intelligence

00:49:00.840 --> 00:49:03.420
about global load
balancing to those proxies.

00:49:03.420 --> 00:49:05.710
So it's a departure
from the traditional way

00:49:05.710 --> 00:49:08.190
where the server side
did the load balancing.

00:49:08.190 --> 00:49:10.320
Now the intelligence is
with the Traffic Director,

00:49:10.320 --> 00:49:11.910
which is feeding
your client side

00:49:11.910 --> 00:49:14.580
proxy to then go pick
the optimal back end

00:49:14.580 --> 00:49:16.050
for the next service.

00:49:16.050 --> 00:49:17.610
So this is what you see.

00:49:17.610 --> 00:49:19.575
And then the reason
it's important is this.

00:49:19.575 --> 00:49:21.900
This exactly matches
what I spoke about

00:49:21.900 --> 00:49:24.330
for global load balancing,
but in the context

00:49:24.330 --> 00:49:25.380
of micro services.

00:49:25.380 --> 00:49:28.260
So you can overflow,
you can do failovers,

00:49:28.260 --> 00:49:32.580
and your services will be much
more resilient in this case.

00:49:32.580 --> 00:49:34.402
And like everything
else, I mean,

00:49:34.402 --> 00:49:36.360
if there was one takeaway
from this conference,

00:49:36.360 --> 00:49:39.420
you already know that Google
is big on Hybrid Cloud.

00:49:39.420 --> 00:49:41.610
And so in the future,
we do want to provide

00:49:41.610 --> 00:49:43.590
Traffic Director
for your work loads

00:49:43.590 --> 00:49:45.240
which are not in Google Cloud.

00:49:45.240 --> 00:49:47.250
And then the last thing
that we also want to do

00:49:47.250 --> 00:49:50.370
is with all of this, we fit
into the CSB architecture.

00:49:50.370 --> 00:49:53.670
So you do have this really
neat and nice platform

00:49:53.670 --> 00:49:55.140
that lets you go
and build services

00:49:55.140 --> 00:49:57.750
at scale and without much toil.

00:49:57.750 --> 00:49:59.342
So it was a whirlwind deep dive.

00:49:59.342 --> 00:50:01.800
But thank you for coming, and
we hope you got something out

00:50:01.800 --> 00:50:02.470
of this session.

00:50:02.470 --> 00:50:02.970
Thank you.

00:50:02.970 --> 00:50:04.170
[APPLAUSE]

00:50:04.170 --> 00:50:07.220
[UPBEAT MUSIC PLAYING]

